abstract: >-
  Companion artifacts and source for the paper "Bishop-Loop: A
  Methodological Post-Mortem on Two-Model Code-Optimization
  Autoloops." Bishop-loop is a two-model code-optimization autoloop
  pairing a capable proposer (qwen3-coder:30b) with a smaller
  idea-generator each iteration, with a single PROMOTE gate selecting
  the better outcome. The work reports three methodological findings
  -- a same-arms degeneracy diagnostic based on arm-to-arm code
  similarity, a calibrated cross-model critique null at n=10 seeds,
  and a quantified edit-format baseline showing 97% / 94% failure
  rates for unified diffs versus 83-86% apply success for aider-style
  SEARCH/REPLACE blocks -- and one architectural result that was
  initially confounded and is now resolved by a pre-registered, fully
  matched skippy_parallel control: holding search budget and edit
  format fixed, the Bishop-directed two-model loop is significantly
  faster than an undirected second draw (Mann-Whitney U p = 0.0005,
  Cliff's delta = 0.52), though the effect is small (1.7% of parse
  time) and a second undirected draw is not itself significantly
  better than single-model best-of-1 (p = 0.072). The archive
  contains the paper source, the JSON-parser benchmark target, the
  autoloop harness, and per-run experimental artifacts.
